1
00:00:00,533 --> 00:00:03,300
The microwave oven started with a ruined snack.

2
00:00:03,633 --> 00:00:10,755
In the mid-nineteen-forties, engineer Percy Spencer was working near a magnetron — the tube that makes radar's waves.

3
00:00:10,855 --> 00:00:14,367
Then he noticed the candy bar in his pocket had melted.

4
00:00:14,767 --> 00:00:16,667
So he tested food on purpose.

5
00:00:16,767 --> 00:00:19,278
Popcorn kernels by the magnetron popped.

6
00:00:19,378 --> 00:00:20,800
An egg exploded.

7
00:00:21,333 --> 00:00:24,903
Then the key step: he concentrated the waves in a small space.

8
00:00:25,003 --> 00:00:27,000
Food placed there heated fast.

9
00:00:27,433 --> 00:00:32,967
In October, nineteen forty-five, Raytheon filed a patent for cooking with microwaves.

10
00:00:33,500 --> 00:00:38,534
Microwave ovens still work that way — a magnetron filling a metal box with waves.

11
00:00:38,634 --> 00:00:44,633
Every time you press Start, you're using the answer to the question a melted candy bar raised.
